/target
*.grim
*.grim.manifest.json

# Gnomish harness runtime: worktrees, logs, run records, progress
.gnomish/
PROGRESS.md

# Build output from a second target dir. 967MB and regenerated by any build.
/target-next

# Build output from the gate's target dir (CARGO_TARGET_DIR=target-wf, which every build and
# test run in this tree uses). Regenerated by any build.
/target-wf

# Build output from the verification target dir. Same reason as the two above.
/target-verify

# Gnomish harness configuration: absolute paths to a build plan and a CLI binary on one
# machine, and nothing the product builds or runs from. Untracked so a clone carries no local
# directory layout.
/gnomish.json

# wasm-pack output. Built from crates/grimoire-wasm, which IS tracked.
/web/*.wasm

# Scratch copies the ingest leaves beside a corpus file it rewrote.
*.before-msg

# THE WIKI CORPUS IS DELIBERATELY NOT COMMITTED.
#
# `crates/grimoire-desktop/data` is ~32MB of content ingested from eqlwiki.com: gear, quest items,
# tooltips, spells, sky, merchants, kills, factions and several hundred item icons. Three reasons it
# stays out of git, and any one of them is enough:
#
#   * IT IS DERIVED. The ingest regenerates it from the wiki, so committing it puts a second copy of
#     somebody else's data under version control with no way to tell which copy is current.
#   * IT IS THIRD PARTY, and this repo is headed open source. Whose licence that content ships under
#     is the owner's decision to make deliberately, and a git history is the wrong place to make it
#     by accident: taking 32MB of scraped content back out of history is a rewrite, not a delete.
#   * NOTHING NEEDS IT TO BUILD. The corpus is runtime content, so a fresh clone compiles.
#
# TESTING A FRESH CLONE NEEDS ONE FLAG, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Eleven tests read the real corpus,
# and they FAIL rather than skip when it is absent, because a suite that goes green on an empty
# machine certifies nothing. The operator says so explicitly instead:
#
#     GRIMOIRE_NO_DATA=1 cargo test --workspace
#
# which prints SKIPPED ON PURPOSE for each one. Measured on a real clone of this commit: 1,045 pass
# and 11 skip. Without the flag, 11 fail and each names the file it wanted.
#
# AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS NOTE CLAIMED A FRESH CLONE WAS SIMPLY GREEN. It is not, and the claim
# was written without cloning. It is corrected here rather than deleted because the difference
# between `needs no data` and `needs one flag` is exactly what the next person will trip on.
#
# The app finds it at runtime through `data::Snapshot::locate()`, overridable by the Data root on
# the Settings screen.
/crates/grimoire-desktop/data

# MINISIGN SECRET KEYS, WHICH MUST NEVER REACH THIS REPOSITORY.
#
# The update client trusts exactly the public keys compiled into `updater::verify::KEYS`, and the
# matching secret is the one thing that can sign a release every shipped binary will then install.
# It lives in a GitHub Actions secret and in offline storage, never here. This pattern is a second
# floor under that rule, not the rule itself: a key committed and then deleted is still in the
# history, and the only remedy at that point is generating a new keypair and abandoning every
# binary already in the field, because a shipped binary cannot be taught a new key.
*.key
